  tldr: 'Income tax return filing for a Gulberg 3 resident or business owner means declaring income — salary, business, commercial rental, or a mix — and reflecting assets in a wealth statement. Salaried return: Rs. 3,500. Business/freelancer return: Rs. 5,000. Fully remote.',
  sections: [
    { id: 'who-files-in-gulberg-3', h: 'Who in Gulberg 3 Needs to File', body: `<p>The same basic rule applies to every registered earner, but Gulberg 3's dense commercial character shifts the mix — business owners and commercial property landlords show up as filers here noticeably more often than they would in a mostly residential neighborhood, alongside the ordinary salaried residents too.</p>` },
    { id: 'what-you-need', h: 'What You Need to File', body: `<ul><li>Your NTN and CNIC.</li><li>Salary certificate or business income and expense summary.</li><li>Commercial or residential property, bank account, and other asset details for the wealth statement.</li></ul>` },
    { id: 'the-filing-process', h: 'The Filing Process, Step by Step', body: `<p>A Gulberg 3 filing follows the standard FBR IRIS route — declared income reconciled against withheld tax, plus a wealth statement for the year. What varies here is the mix: an agency owner's business income sits alongside commercial rental figures more often than in a purely residential area, so the reconciliation covers a few more moving parts than a single-income salaried filing would.</p>` },
    { id: 'commercial-landlord-filing', h: 'Filing as a Gulberg 3 Commercial Property Landlord', body: `<p>A Gulberg 3 owner renting out commercial space — an office, a retail unit, a floor in a plaza — needs to declare that rental income alongside any other income when filing, and the property itself is reflected as an asset in the wealth statement at its recorded value.</p>` },
    { id: 'professional-services-filing', h: 'Filing for Professional Service Providers Based in Gulberg 3', body: `<p>Gulberg 3 hosts many independent professionals — consultants, doctors, lawyers, agencies — often operating from a rented office. Their professional income is declared through their personal return the same way as any self-employed filer's income would be.</p>` },
    { id: 'common-mistakes-gulberg-3', h: 'Mistakes That Come Up for Gulberg 3 Filers', body: `<p>The most frequent issue is a commercial property landlord failing to declare full rental income where a portion is collected informally or in cash — this is exactly the kind of gap that a wealth statement mismatch can eventually surface.</p>` },
    { id: 'after-filing', h: 'What Happens After You File', body: `<p>Appearing on the Active Taxpayer List after processing is what actually unlocks the lower withholding rate, and in Gulberg 3, where both commercial and residential values run high, that difference shows up meaningfully on a landlord's or agency owner's next transaction.</p>` },
    { id: 'agency-and-firm-filing-gulberg-3', h: 'Filing for Gulberg 3 Agencies and Small Firms', body: `<p>Gulberg 3 hosts a fair number of small agencies — marketing, real estate, recruitment — often structured as a proprietorship or a small partnership rather than a full company. A proprietorship's income flows through the owner's personal return as usual, while a partnership (AOP) structure has its own separate filing considerations, with each partner also filing individually based on their agreed profit share — worth confirming which structure actually applies to your specific setup.</p>` },
    { id: 'mixed-use-property-gulberg-3', h: 'Filing for a Mixed-Use Gulberg 3 Property', body: `<p>Some Gulberg 3 buildings combine ground-floor commercial space with residential floors above, all under one ownership. Filing for a mixed-use property means separating out commercial rental income from any residential use accurately, since the two can carry different practical considerations even though both ultimately get declared together in the same overall return.</p>` },
    { id: 'multiple-tenants-gulberg-3', h: 'Declaring Income From Multiple Gulberg 3 Tenants', body: `<p>A Gulberg 3 landlord renting out several units — whether in the same building or across different properties — declares the combined rental income from all tenants together, rather than needing a separate filing per unit or per tenant. Keeping a simple record of what each unit brings in over the year makes totaling this accurately at filing time considerably easier.</p>` },
    { id: 'first-time-professional-filers-gulberg-3', h: 'Filing for the First Time as a Gulberg 3 Professional', body: `<p>A newly independent consultant or professional setting up in Gulberg 3 for the first time — having just left salaried employment, for instance — files quite differently from their old salaried returns. Business income and legitimate professional expenses now need to be tracked and declared, rather than relying on a single salary certificate as before, and getting comfortable with this shift early avoids under- or over-declaring in that first year of independent practice.</p>` },
    { id: 'currency-and-forex-gulberg-3', h: 'Filing for Gulberg 3 Businesses With Foreign Currency Transactions', body: `<p>Some Gulberg 3 agencies and consultancies bill international clients in foreign currency, which means income needs to be converted to Pakistani rupees at an appropriate rate for filing purposes rather than declared in the original currency. Keeping a consistent, defensible conversion approach across the year — rather than an inconsistent mix of rates — makes for a cleaner, more easily reconciled return.</p>` },
    { id: 'commercial-vacancy-gulberg-3', h: 'Filing When a Gulberg 3 Commercial Unit Sits Vacant Part of the Year', body: `<p>A Gulberg 3 landlord whose commercial unit was vacant for part of the tax year — between tenants, or during a renovation — simply declares whatever rental income was actually collected during the months it was occupied, rather than an estimated full-year figure. The property itself still needs reflecting as an asset in the wealth statement regardless of occupancy status.</p>` },
    { id: 'staff-and-payroll-gulberg-3', h: 'Declaring Staff Costs for a Gulberg 3 Agency or Firm', body: `<p>A Gulberg 3 agency or small firm employing staff should track and declare salary and wage costs as a legitimate business expense, reducing net taxable profit the same way rent or utilities would. Where staff are formally on payroll with their own tax withholding, this also needs to be handled correctly on the employer's side, separate from but related to the business's own annual filing.</p>` },
    { id: 'kamboh-help', h: 'How Kamboh Associates Helps Gulberg 3 Filers', body: `<p>We handle income tax return filing for Gulberg 3 residents, business owners, and commercial landlords entirely over WhatsApp. Rs. 3,500 salaried, Rs. 5,000 business/freelancer.</p>` }
  ],
